### SB-MIN-002 — transform() else-clause invariant assertion

`scripts/enable-required-check.sh`: in `transform()`, the `TOP_LEVEL_TOGGLE_KEYS` branch has the shape:

```python
elif key in TOP_LEVEL_TOGGLE_KEYS:
    if isinstance(value, dict) and set(value.keys()) <= {"enabled", "url"} and isinstance(value.get("enabled"), bool):
        out[key] = bool(value["enabled"])
    elif isinstance(value, bool):
        out[key] = value
    # else: malformed top-level toggle shape; drop from PUT body (validator-enforced contract)
```

The validator added in fix-round-1 now rejects malformed shapes before `transform()` runs, so the implicit drop should be unreachable. Replace the trailing comment with an explicit else clause that raises an exception (assertion-style):

```python
elif key in TOP_LEVEL_TOGGLE_KEYS:
    if isinstance(value, dict) and set(value.keys()) <= {"enabled", "url"} and isinstance(value.get("enabled"), bool):
        out[key] = bool(value["enabled"])
    elif isinstance(value, bool):
        out[key] = value
    else:
        # Validator precondition violated; should be unreachable.
        print(
            f"internal error: transform() reached unreachable branch for '{key}'; "
            f"validator should have rejected this shape upstream",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        sys.exit(2)
```

This makes the invariant explicit and prevents silent drop if the validator is ever weakened.

